1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,220
There is no lead in your pencil.

2
00:00:02,838 --> 00:00:03,888
There never was.

3
00:00:04,408 --> 00:00:06,238
That grey line is graphite.

4
00:00:06,968 --> 00:00:10,038
In four minutes, a tree and
a rock become this pencil.

5
00:00:10,578 --> 00:00:14,008
On the way, they pass through an
oven at about eleven hundred degrees.

6
00:00:14,618 --> 00:00:17,928
And it all hangs on a glued
seam you've never noticed.

7
00:00:18,668 --> 00:00:19,898
Let's start with the name.

8
00:00:21,278 --> 00:00:22,548
So why do we call it lead?

9
00:00:23,148 --> 00:00:28,208
In the fifteen hundreds, a pure black
rock turned up in Borrowdale, in northern

10
00:00:28,228 --> 00:00:29,278
England.

11
00:00:29,388 --> 00:00:32,518
It was so pure, it could be
sawn straight into sticks.

12
00:00:33,168 --> 00:00:37,118
It looked like lead and marked like
lead, so people called it black lead.

13
00:00:37,688 --> 00:00:40,878
In seventeen seventy-nine,
it turned out to be carbon.

14
00:00:41,418 --> 00:00:46,008
Ten years later it was named
graphite, from the Greek for to write.

15
00:00:46,448 --> 00:00:49,038
Then war cut France off
from English graphite.

16
00:00:49,438 --> 00:00:54,638
So in seventeen ninety-five, Nicolas-Jacques
Conté mixed powdered graphite with

17
00:00:54,658 --> 00:00:56,228
clay, and baked it.

18
00:00:56,618 --> 00:00:58,668
Pencil cores are still made that way.

19
00:01:00,058 --> 00:01:04,268
Zoom into that grey line, and
something strange appears.

20
00:01:04,908 --> 00:01:09,638
Graphite is carbon atoms, locked
into flat sheets of hexagons.

21
00:01:10,218 --> 00:01:13,488
In a sheet, each atom grips
three neighbours tightly.

22
00:01:14,078 --> 00:01:16,148
But the sheets are only loosely stacked.

23
00:01:16,718 --> 00:01:20,888
Press down and drag, and the top sheets
slide off, like cards from a deck.

24
00:01:21,378 --> 00:01:25,208
Those loose flakes catch in the
paper fibres. That's your line.

25
00:01:26,008 --> 00:01:29,118
Here's the twist: a diamond
is made of the same atoms.

26
00:01:29,668 --> 00:01:34,338
Same carbon. One is the hardest
mineral, the other one of the softest.

27
00:01:34,768 --> 00:01:37,938
Pure graphite, though, is
too soft to work on its own.

28
00:01:39,258 --> 00:01:43,008
So the core starts as two
powders: graphite and clay.

29
00:01:43,598 --> 00:01:47,508
Add water, and they're ground
together into a smooth grey paste.

30
00:01:48,068 --> 00:01:52,318
The paste is squeezed through a
tiny hole into long, thin strings.

31
00:01:52,968 --> 00:01:55,418
The strings are cut to
length and left to dry.

32
00:01:55,908 --> 00:01:59,858
Then they go into a kiln, at about
eleven hundred degrees Celsius.

33
00:02:00,598 --> 00:02:02,768
The heat bakes the clay into hard cores.

34
00:02:03,398 --> 00:02:07,428
A dip in hot wax fills the
pores, so the core glides.

35
00:02:08,308 --> 00:02:10,998
So why do some pencils
write darker than others?

36
00:02:11,628 --> 00:02:12,858
It's all in the recipe.

37
00:02:13,498 --> 00:02:17,888
More graphite: a softer
core, and a darker line.

38
00:02:18,418 --> 00:02:22,508
More clay: a harder
core, and a lighter line.

39
00:02:23,008 --> 00:02:28,238
Most scales run from nine H, the
hardest, to nine B, the softest.

40
00:02:28,738 --> 00:02:32,368
H B is in the middle: in the
US, the number two pencil.

41
00:02:33,748 --> 00:02:37,458
Now, the wood. And the core
still has to get inside it.

42
00:02:37,808 --> 00:02:39,918
Many pencils are made from incense cedar.

43
00:02:40,428 --> 00:02:44,178
The logs are cut into
blocks, then into thin slats.

44
00:02:44,758 --> 00:02:50,128
Each slat is about eighteen centimetres
long, and just over half a centimetre thick.

45
00:02:50,598 --> 00:02:53,688
The slats are dried, stained and waxed.

46
00:02:54,008 --> 00:02:57,258
Then a cutter carves parallel
grooves along each slat.

47
00:02:57,658 --> 00:02:59,988
Each groove is only
half as deep as a core.

48
00:03:00,598 --> 00:03:03,668
Why half? Because every
core gets two slats.

49
00:03:05,038 --> 00:03:08,548
Glue goes on first. Then a
core drops into every groove.

50
00:03:09,078 --> 00:03:12,888
A second slat lands on top, and
the stack goes into a press.

51
00:03:13,478 --> 00:03:15,488
The press holds it until the glue sets.

52
00:03:16,308 --> 00:03:18,418
So your pencil was never one piece.

53
00:03:19,018 --> 00:03:20,668
It's two halves, glued together.

54
00:03:21,268 --> 00:03:24,398
That glue line runs the full
length of every wooden pencil.

55
00:03:24,918 --> 00:03:27,288
Cutters shape the sandwich into hexagons.

56
00:03:27,908 --> 00:03:30,238
Six to nine pencils from every sandwich.

57
00:03:30,778 --> 00:03:35,648
Two wooden halves glued around a core is
an old idea. It's still how it's done.

58
00:03:36,458 --> 00:03:40,048
But right now, these pencils
are bare wood. They need colour.

59
00:03:41,448 --> 00:03:43,718
Each pencil gets coat after coat of paint.

60
00:03:44,178 --> 00:03:46,568
Layer by layer, a smooth, hard shell.

61
00:03:47,188 --> 00:03:49,058
The eraser has its own story.

62
00:03:49,628 --> 00:03:54,328
In eighteen fifty-eight, a Philadelphia
stationer patented a pencil with an attached

63
00:03:54,348 --> 00:03:55,398
eraser.

64
00:03:55,608 --> 00:03:59,618
Seventeen years later, the US Supreme
Court declared that patent invalid.

65
00:04:00,168 --> 00:04:04,338
Today, a metal ferrule is clamped
on, and the eraser is pushed inside.

66
00:04:04,948 --> 00:04:07,078
One last stamp marks the grade.

67
00:04:07,608 --> 00:04:13,148
Two cedar halves. A baked core.
One glue line. Paint, metal

68
00:04:13,168 --> 00:04:14,218
and rubber.

69
00:04:15,188 --> 00:04:19,798
A tree and a rock, through
the fire, and into your hand.

70
00:04:20,368 --> 00:04:23,678
And every line it draws
is graphite. Never lead.

71
00:04:25,068 --> 00:04:29,008
Next, how a ballpoint pen works.
